\paragraph{Independence-accuracy trade-off.} The failure pattern becomes interpretable through $\kappa$ generalization analysis. Table~\ref{tab:kappa_generalization} compares Fleiss' $\kappa$ across three aggregation methods on Mistral-7B FOLIO ($n = 204$). Self-Consistency produces $\kappa = 0.44$ (Eff-$K = 2.02$, nominal $K = 10$). Diverse-Prompt, which varies the instruction prompt across traces, achieves $\kappa = 0.40$ (Eff-$K = 2.21$, nominal $K = 12$): prompt variation does not break the underlying correlation. Debate achieves $\kappa = 0.15$ (Eff-$K = 4.44$), nearly tripling the effective sample size, but collapses accuracy by $-18.6$\,pp. This constitutes a fundamental trade-off: methods that increase trace independence (Debate) destroy the accuracy that makes aggregation useful, while methods that preserve accuracy (ICG, Diverse-Prompt) cannot break the correlation floor. The trade-off is a Pareto frontier inherent to same-model aggregation, not an engineering gap. The only demonstrated path past this frontier requires signal from outside the model's own distribution, consistent with the oracle result (\S\ref{sec:oracle}) and with published systems that bypass self-extraction \citep{xu2026dylos, li2025aristotle, wang2025fover, pan2023logiclm}.

