This design functions as a causal intervention on constraint quality. If the oracle variant produces large gains while self-extraction does not, the aggregation mechanism itself is sound, and the failure localizes to constraint extraction. Conversely, if the oracle also fails, the MAX-SAT aggregation framework is insufficient regardless of constraint quality. Results of this intervention appear in \S\ref{sec:oracle}.

\subsection{Scope}
\label{sec:scope}

SICA as evaluated here uses \emph{same-model self-extraction}: the same LLM generates traces and extracts constraints. This design isolates whether a model's own reasoning can be usefully formalized for aggregation. Methods that use trained extractors, external provers, or multi-model ensembles fall outside the scope of this evaluation. We test twelve remediation strategies: nine prompt-level spanning six dimensions (prompting, architecture, capability, verification, diversity, filtering) and three method-level (ICG, Contrastive, Debate) targeting different aspects of the independence bottleneck (\S\ref{sec:remediation}).

